24:42.205 --> 24:50.453
[SPEAKER_00]: flying for nine to 10 hours at a stretch, they wear compression socks to prevent the blood pooling into their feet and their feet swelling up and

24:50.473 --> 24:58.276
[SPEAKER_00]: all that. But that's for slightly different reasons compared to handling G-forces. Insofar as handling G-forces is concerned, two

24:58.337 --> 25:06.261
[SPEAKER_00]: other things can be done, which are also practiced by fighter pilots who handle high G-forces. It's called the Valsalva Mural, wherein you

25:06.421 --> 25:14.261
[SPEAKER_00]: tighten your stomach muscles and your thigh muscles really hard with a sharp breath. that helps you improve your g tolerance and prevents

25:14.421 --> 25:22.443
[SPEAKER_00]: blood flow downward from your brain towards the lower parts of the body so that is something i can foresee may be helpful for aqua pirates

25:23.084 --> 25:31.706
[SPEAKER_00]: tightening of the stomach muscles tightening of the thigh muscles and a little shout to help that tightening with the g tolerances once again as

25:31.746 --> 25:43.207
[SPEAKER_00]: far as i know most g tolerances even in the I think actual maneuvers are mostly still along the z-axis, head to your toe. And for that you can do

25:43.247 --> 25:52.772
[SPEAKER_00]: something. For all other g maneuvers which happen maybe in a twisting motion or a turning motion, may not be handled well even with all these

25:52.792 --> 25:53.153
[SPEAKER_00]: maneuvers.

25:57.772 --> 26:04.958
[SPEAKER_01]: I think the fact this makes a man perfect is the definition that applies here. The more you expose yourself to such forces, the better equipped

