[SPEAKER_00]: learning. That I'm going to go there and I'm going to meet a bunch of people and we will have discussions on subjects in which I will learn

35:45.243 --> 35:54.203
[SPEAKER_00]: from them. And it happens in the flow of the general banter, that kind of thing. If you want a support group which is focused for improving your

35:54.223 --> 36:02.626
[SPEAKER_00]: flying, for improving your knowledge of the sport, then there are many other ways to do it. And it doesn't stem from something like this. Let's

36:02.646 --> 36:14.270
[SPEAKER_01]: say when we have figured out a comfort zone where we are able to bring our troubles and triumphs to a certain set of people, which is going to take a

36:14.310 --> 36:25.191
[SPEAKER_01]: lot of trust and energy to reach A place in life where you trust someone, that you give them a lead in things which matter to you in the most

36:25.271 --> 36:34.615
[SPEAKER_01]: intimate manner. Identification of a toxic relationship or ground suck as we call it in paragliding, especially when you're new,

36:34.875 --> 36:43.317
[SPEAKER_01]: especially when you're still in the early hours of your airtime. how do you identify ground suck because i can tell you from my own experience i

36:43.357 --> 36:53.647
[SPEAKER_01]: have trusted instructors which were not worth being given an instructor's license a lot of that was in my early few hours because you

36:53.707 --> 37:00.394
[SPEAKER_01]: have just discovered this brand new fascination that somebody's saying hey i'm professional in this pay me and i'll give you the feelings

37:00.414 --> 37:08.376
[SPEAKER_01]: that you're looking for And of course, the vulnerabilities are quite high. But still, if you have to identify toxic relationships in these

37:08.796 --> 37:16.800
[SPEAKER_01]: support groups, what's your way? What are your thoughts on that? And how does one basically avoid the pitfalls that are in those pathways?

37:17.741 --> 37:27.383
[SPEAKER_00]: Interestingly, I'll bring you something from my experiences of doing a lot of tricking and climbing in the past. And I always say that if you want

37:27.423 --> 37:35.704
[SPEAKER_00]: to see how good a person is, you should take him along on a nice, long, difficult trek. You will get to understand what kind of person he is

37:35.744 --> 37:47.106
[SPEAKER_00]: because when you're in trouble together and the hardships are present at all times, you will see a person in true light. So physical stress,

37:47.287 --> 37:58.247
[SPEAKER_00]: mental stress, and for prolonged periods actually bring out the worst in you. And it's at that time you'll understand the true nativities of

37:58.267 --> 38:10.656
[SPEAKER_00]: what a person is all about. So there may be a lot of people who may be knowledgeable, who may be good players and pilots themselves, but they

38:10.696 --> 38:23.201
[SPEAKER_00]: may not make good teachers. Because teaching itself is an art. The ability to judge what mistake a person is making And to be able to analyze

38:23.241 --> 38:35.204
[SPEAKER_00]: it to the extent of being able to give you ready-to-action statements to fix that problem, that is a big art. Anybody can tell you, oh, your

38:35.224 --> 38:44.387
[SPEAKER_00]: lighting technique is not good. You're flaring high. But can someone tell you, why are you flaring high? Why? You're flaring high. It's a

38:44.427 --> 38:52.544
[SPEAKER_00]: mistake. Fix it. Don't flare high. Flare low. Or flare the right height. But why would you be making that mistake? That's where a good instructor

38:52.564 --> 38:59.248
[SPEAKER_00]: will come in. He will tell you, listen, you're making this mistake because of this and this reason. In order not to do this, you should do

38:59.428 --> 39:11.297
[SPEAKER_00]: concrete steps, A, B, C, and D. Actionable steps. So this art of being able to pinpoint why you're doing something and how to fix it is a

39:11.597 --> 39:21.876
[SPEAKER_00]: critical part. You may not be able to realize this as an amateur pilot. So you have no option but to take other people's inputs. What we tend to do in

39:21.956 --> 39:30.523
[SPEAKER_00]: the modern world is look at Instagram clips by people or video by people or to say, oh, this person is a very knowledgeable person. Like I said,

39:30.784 --> 39:37.830
[SPEAKER_00]: being knowledgeable or being a good pilot, you may not be a good instructor. That's an important thing to understand. Once you do

39:37.890 --> 39:47.274
[SPEAKER_00]: understand that there is a problem in a particular person with whom you are with, nothing prevents you from pulling away and finding somebody

39:47.334 --> 39:53.736
[SPEAKER_00]: else to teach him or somebody else to be with whom you're more comfortable or you would learn better with. The teacher could be good or

39:53.756 --> 39:59.957
[SPEAKER_00]: the student could be good, but they may not be good together. Let's say you as a student don't learn well unless somebody really hammers

39:59.997 --> 40:06.299
[SPEAKER_00]: something down your head. And here's a teacher who's very meek who refuses to do that. Or the other way around. The teacher is extremely

40:06.319 --> 40:13.440
[SPEAKER_00]: violent in teaching, shouting and screaming at you. And you're a meek person. You don't like somebody who is voicing, teaching you. And then

40:13.460 --> 40:21.090
[SPEAKER_00]: there's a conversation you both feel, despite being good I love the way you put it. It's very clear. And I think this is another one of those

40:21.130 --> 40:28.054
[SPEAKER_01]: questions which people will have to find an answer themselves, which is a bit difficult in the beginning of your career because you're still

40:28.114 --> 40:39.841
[SPEAKER_01]: learning the tricks of the trade. But eventually, learning to question your instructors and seeing their replies is what will probably give

40:39.881 --> 40:48.217
[SPEAKER_01]: you balanced ground to stand on and then take your decisions from there on. One thing I can argue is something that will indicate that

40:48.237 --> 40:58.361
[SPEAKER_00]: everything is okay. And that is when somebody is ready to teach you and give you all his knowledge and all that he's learned from his experience

40:58.921 --> 41:07.765
[SPEAKER_00]: without holding anything back. Let's assume that on a particular subject, I know 10 things as bits and pieces of knowledge and 10

41:08.886 --> 41:16.866
[SPEAKER_00]: experiences I've had, people can learn something about that particular topic. I want to teach you only four and withhold six. from

41:17.006 --> 41:26.839
[SPEAKER_00]: either lot then there's a problem the question is are you able to discern that you'll be given only six out of ten and not the whole ten that's part

41:26.919 --> 41:34.468
[SPEAKER_00]: one that you could make out because you had a discussion or topic in which you already knew a lot and you wanted to check if this person is passing on

41:34.488 --> 41:43.123
[SPEAKER_00]: that information to you or not And the other one is that you can make out by his mannerisms and body language that he's hiding something because he

41:43.163 --> 41:49.648
[SPEAKER_00]: wants to say something and he's holding himself or she wants to say something, he's holding something back. And if you're a good human

41:49.708 --> 41:58.415
[SPEAKER_00]: observer, these things do become visible after a certain amount of time. Let's try and look at it from the perspective of the instructors

41:58.475 --> 42:06.076
[SPEAKER_01]: who don't want to share everything just for the sake of it. Let's put ourselves in their shoes. Where this person is like, if I open all my

42:06.116 --> 42:14.801
[SPEAKER_01]: cards in the beginning, then I am losing out on money that is coming on later. I understand a lot of this can be blamed on capitalism, that I have

42:14.841 --> 42:23.845
[SPEAKER_01]: to make money in a capitalist society and I can't give them all the knowledge for whatever amount they have agreed on. Does it make sense to

42:24.485 --> 42:35.955
[SPEAKER_01]: give a person only module one when the person wants all three modules in a go, for example, from the instructor's point of view, purely because of

42:35.995 --> 42:48.506
[SPEAKER_01]: monetary benefit? So it's definitely a monetary issue. How long can you be in a position where you need the instructor to constantly pay money

42:48.586 --> 42:57.742
[SPEAKER_00]: for learning something? And there, as long as you're holding things back, I don't blame somebody for making money. But the thing is, if

42:57.762 --> 43:05.807
