[SPEAKER_00]: front of my eyes, in very close proximity. And that has a very powerful effect on me. and to understand and bear that knowing that somebody has

46:52.630 --> 47:00.736
[SPEAKER_00]: lost his legs in the big fireball in front has a very strong powerful effect and it makes you learn so it has and it has a way of molding your

47:00.776 --> 47:11.663
[SPEAKER_00]: attitude toward what you're doing in my case that what you mentioned was me losing my wife last year in a targeting accident she was reasonably

47:11.703 --> 47:19.707
[SPEAKER_00]: experienced by getting with about the same amount of flying experience that i had She had a very bad collapse in Bir, which resulted in her

47:19.747 --> 47:28.416
[SPEAKER_00]: hitting the ground and as a consequence, she passed away. It happened in front of me. I was very close to her when this happened. And the ridge on

47:28.456 --> 47:36.921
[SPEAKER_00]: which she had gone down was a pretty steep ridge and pretty sharp on its sides and the upper parts. It's the ridge which people call the golf

47:36.961 --> 47:45.567
[SPEAKER_00]: course ridge. The area lower down the ridge where people do top land and they hang around there. It happened much higher up on the same ridge at a

47:45.627 --> 47:53.873
[SPEAKER_00]: place where the ridge was rocky. When this happened, we were talking about how to handle something like this. I was initially completely

47:54.093 --> 48:03.620
[SPEAKER_00]: aghast in seeing the glider go down. And initially, I made a lot of calls on the radio. I started flying towards where she was. Not very far. Maybe

48:03.901 --> 48:11.725
[SPEAKER_00]: about 800 meters to a kilometer. I came closer to where she was. I gave a lot of calls. There was no response on the radio by anybody. So my initial

48:11.745 --> 48:21.551
[SPEAKER_00]: instinct was I should go land. I said, no, hold on. Let's assume I mess up my landing. I break a leg. My wife is there. I haven't reached. Nobody is

48:21.591 --> 48:28.032
[SPEAKER_00]: responding on the radio. Nobody knows that she's down because otherwise somebody would have responded. Now I go down. And I break a

48:28.052 --> 48:34.774
[SPEAKER_00]: leg. And now we're going to have an emergency. Two people have crashed in the mountain. Nobody knows where they are. Presumably when I crash, I'm

48:34.794 --> 48:42.856
[SPEAKER_00]: still conscious and I can use the radio and shout that I have a broken leg and my wife is higher up in the mountain and needs rescuing or whatever. I

48:42.876 --> 48:51.538
[SPEAKER_00]: said, no, it doesn't make sense. It's only after somebody responded, another senior pilot, another two pilots came and landed at Gulf Coast

48:51.638 --> 48:59.292
[SPEAKER_00]: Point on the ridge is when I attempted to land there and land there. And then, of course, we all ran up. Another senior pilot, a lot more

48:59.332 --> 49:06.473
[SPEAKER_00]: experienced than all of us, landed at a slightly higher point and managed to reach my wife first. And we landed up there. It was already too

49:06.513 --> 49:15.856
[SPEAKER_00]: late. She had died from that crash because she went straight into a rock. And that caused her death. Now, the point is, what do you do after

49:15.896 --> 49:24.818
[SPEAKER_00]: something like this has happened? You're losing somebody close in your family. But there's another dimension to this is that I fly. Our son also

49:24.838 --> 49:33.681
[SPEAKER_00]: flies. And I know I can't remove evasion from my life. I can't remove bad reading from my life. And most in respect of her having lost her life,

49:33.741 --> 49:44.249
[SPEAKER_00]: being the same. And I don't want this to become a constant fear for my son, for him to ever develop a state wherein he gives up on such a rewarding and

49:44.770 --> 49:55.995
[SPEAKER_00]: nice sport. So I said the only way to handle this is to get back in the air. And I must do it while my son is still there. So I think within about two

49:56.035 --> 50:05.098
[SPEAKER_00]: weeks of the incident i told my son monday i think i'm gonna go fly and he didn't say anything he didn't try and stop me or anything so i went up and

50:05.738 --> 50:13.360
[SPEAKER_00]: did a flight i didn't fly too long it was nearly a top-down flight from takeoff to landing by about 15-20 minutes i could sense the

50:13.380 --> 50:22.339
[SPEAKER_00]: apprehension within my body i would keep turning and looking towards the place where she had gone down into the ground And it definitely was, I

50:22.379 --> 50:30.841
[SPEAKER_00]: wouldn't say stressful, but it really blew my attention away from the actual act of flying. And I realized that this is disconcerting. But you

50:30.861 --> 50:39.163
[SPEAKER_00]: can't run away from your fears. If you have a fear, you have to learn to handle it. And you have to take it head on. Otherwise, that small fear

50:39.183 --> 50:46.945
[SPEAKER_00]: will become into a monster and become something that will prevent you from doing anything in life. So I knew I had to get back to it. Slowly, I

50:46.965 --> 50:55.600
[SPEAKER_00]: started flying more and more. Even though I was doing pretty long flights earlier, I went back to becoming a beginner. First, I think six

50:55.620 --> 51:05.044
[SPEAKER_00]: or seven flights only, just top down. By the sixth or seventh flight, I went to the same golf course region. Maybe the 10th or 15th flight, I went

51:05.184 --> 51:13.628
[SPEAKER_00]: one more reach beyond that to progressively build up my mindset. The probability of the same thing happening immediately in the same area is

51:13.708 --> 51:22.128
[SPEAKER_00]: very remote. Most of the fears are irrational, actually speaking. But yet they are there and they need to be handled. They need to be handled in a

51:22.768 --> 51:31.830
[SPEAKER_00]: manner and to address it in bits and pieces rather than, come on, let's get in there and do something. That's how you handle this sort of thing. I

51:31.870 --> 51:39.492
[SPEAKER_01]: wish I could show the amount of strength that is there on your face while speaking about this, Ashutosh. Massive thanks for being so unfiltered

51:39.512 --> 51:47.247
[SPEAKER_01]: and honest about this. It takes a lot of courage. revisit those memories and bring out your new perspective on it. More power to you, sir. You are

51:47.267 --> 51:54.271
[SPEAKER_01]: an inspiration to say the least. And I'm pretty sure a lot of people in our audiences can also resonate to this. Keep rocking, man. Keep rocking.

51:54.491 --> 52:04.131
[SPEAKER_01]: The world needs more people like you. Thanks. One thing I've heard about, but please correct me if I'm wrong, is that in a squadron of

52:04.411 --> 52:16.354
[SPEAKER_01]: fighter jets, if unfortunately one flyer goes down and does not come back, the whole squadron flies the very next day. Why is this? And what is

52:16.394 --> 52:26.096
[SPEAKER_01]: the benefit of doing this? Actually, it's exactly the same as what I did. Like I told you that within 10 days of me losing my wife in that piloting

52:26.156 --> 52:35.411
[SPEAKER_00]: accident, I went back into the air. It went up to 10 days because I had to go away to Delhi. Because I came back to be 10 days later is the reason there

52:35.431 --> 52:43.697
[SPEAKER_00]: was a delay. If I was going to be in Bir and all activities concerning bus or passing away were to happen in Bir, I would have probably flown the

52:43.737 --> 52:53.525
[SPEAKER_00]: very next day. Providing that there was time to do so between all the events and activities. The faster the better. In my case, if I decide not

52:53.545 --> 53:02.027
[SPEAKER_00]: to pilot, you don't pilot. End of story. But pilot pilots don't have the option. You are a part of the Air Force. You know that tomorrow you're

53:02.047 --> 53:11.531
[SPEAKER_00]: going to go to war. You know that every time you see somebody going down and dying, which could happen every day in the war, multiple people

53:11.551 --> 53:20.654
[SPEAKER_00]: could be losing their lives in one day. You can't just say, I won't fly. Then you would face being charged with cowardice and maybe forbutanous

53:20.734 --> 53:28.705
[SPEAKER_00]: action. That's not acceptable for your line of duty. So that's done. We have done it too when I was in the Air Force. When it's happened,

53:28.885 --> 53:35.889
[SPEAKER_00]: something like this, you fly. There is no stopping. And you shouldn't either. Like I said, you don't want that thing that was a small bit of fear

53:36.469 --> 53:43.233
[SPEAKER_00]: now turn its head and turn into a monster. And which it will. It becomes such a mental block that it's impossible to get out of. So the faster it's

53:43.273 --> 53:50.396
[SPEAKER_00]: handled, the better. But remember, when handling it, it should be done in pieces. Don't do something stupid. Try to do something when your mind

53:50.476 --> 54:01.096
[SPEAKER_00]: is affected. And you know that you're not giving your mind 100% when trying to handle something like this. To sum it up, it's basically make a

54:01.116 --> 54:10.280
[SPEAKER_01]: plan in cool minds and then make sure you act on it. Because the longer you sit with the emotion, the worse it's going to get, the harder it's going

54:10.300 --> 54:21.423
[SPEAKER_01]: to get to come out of it. So make a plan, act on it, and then life starts getting better. Handling fear is a big topic by itself. Interestingly,

54:21.583 --> 54:32.693
[SPEAKER_00]: sure, handling fear is very individual. The only thing I can say about being generic is to ask yourself, is this fear grounded? Or is this fear

54:32.913 --> 54:42.461
[SPEAKER_00]: irrational? If the fear is irrational, then there's a big problem because that means your mind is not straight. You need to get your mind

54:42.481 --> 54:53.523
[SPEAKER_00]: straight. If the fear is rational, For example, somebody tells you, listen, take this diagram, right? And we're going to break three of the

54:53.563 --> 55:01.807
[SPEAKER_00]: lines and now you go fly. And now you have fear. That's very rational fear, yeah? Because the probability of something going wrong has

55:02.247 --> 55:11.748
[SPEAKER_00]: increased so much. Now the question is, what if you have to do it? For example, you crash landed somewhere. You broke those lines, all right?

55:12.128 --> 55:17.470
[SPEAKER_00]: And those lines tore in a manner that they cannot be repaired. For example, they tore in multiple pieces and joining them together

55:17.490 --> 55:24.692
[SPEAKER_00]: doesn't work or the fabric itself is torn. And you're stranded from somewhere where you cannot walk down. There's no option but to take off

55:24.993 --> 55:33.216
[SPEAKER_00]: and get everyone back from there. So now you have no option but to fly that glider. Now there's fear in your mind. There is no option, but that's the

55:33.236 --> 55:44.882
[SPEAKER_00]: only option. Now what? So now that fear needs to be handled. So now you have to sit down and think. What is this fear about? What can go wrong? Do I

55:44.922 --> 55:53.148
[SPEAKER_00]: have a plan for what can go wrong? Or is there no plan? There may be some variables which can be handled by you, but some cannot be handled by you.

55:53.168 --> 56:02.434
[SPEAKER_00]: They're completely out of your control. For example, there's a tear in the fabric, right? And the tear is at a point where some portion is torn

56:02.875 --> 56:10.657
[SPEAKER_00]: and the tear cannot possibly extend any further. Quantified problem. This is what's going to happen. It won't tear any further. But if the tear

56:10.697 --> 56:17.680
[SPEAKER_00]: is such a magnitude, the moment the glider gets loaded with your weight, it's going to rip another two feet. You've got a problem that you have no

56:17.820 --> 56:25.504
[SPEAKER_00]: answer for. You don't have any control on the tearing or non-tearing of the fabric at that point. When you need to handle these pairs, you need to

56:25.704 --> 56:32.242
[SPEAKER_00]: think very carefully. of the variables in control and the variables completely outside of control and decide whether your fear is

56:32.802 --> 56:42.184
[SPEAKER_00]: reasonable. A little bit fear, of course, always good. It keeps you alive. I love the way you put it, Ashutosh. Any one of our audiences who is

56:43.084 --> 56:49.185
[SPEAKER_01]: finding this meaningful, go ahead, hit that rewind button for 15 seconds or 20 seconds. Listen to it all over again, because this is pure

56:49.225 --> 56:57.043
[SPEAKER_01]: gold. As you said, not a lot of people are willing to share it or in the most efficient manner, which is understandable. So identifying your

56:57.083 --> 57:08.108
[SPEAKER_01]: reality and then making a plan out of it as a way to move forward in life. I think that is a simple conclusion in here. It reminds me of an incident in

57:08.208 --> 57:17.133
[SPEAKER_01]: Bede. There was a fighter jet pilot flying his paraglider in the Black Mountains and he trash landed there, had to survive out in the

57:17.173 --> 57:27.321
[SPEAKER_01]: wilderness for almost one week in a row. But of course, military training, they figure out how to survive better than civilians and

57:27.461 --> 57:37.585
[SPEAKER_01]: whatever willpower to live. But by the time the rescuers found him, he was actually planning to take off with a glider that was very difficult

57:37.625 --> 57:45.468
[SPEAKER_01]: to fly. That's why I was able to match it with your description. He had quite a few fractures. He had a broken back. Walking out was not an

57:45.508 --> 57:53.991
[SPEAKER_01]: option. The glider was damaged. But flying out was the only survival option for him after waiting for rescue for seven days. Eventually they

57:54.031 --> 58:03.352
[SPEAKER_01]: found him. But that's a whole different story for a new show because in process of finding him, one person lost his life in the rescue effort.

58:03.432 --> 58:14.578
[SPEAKER_01]: But yeah, deep talks, Ashutosh. I think we have come across a whole circle starting from why aviation matters the most and how to make sure

58:14.698 --> 58:22.419
[SPEAKER_01]: it still matters the most regardless of what comes in your life. I think Before we close, I usually have a tradition on my show where I ask my

58:22.459 --> 58:32.228
[SPEAKER_01]: guests to share one flight. And with you, it's a privilege because you have had 14,000 hours in various aircrafts at various altitudes in

58:32.308 --> 58:42.418
[SPEAKER_01]: atmosphere and stratosphere. I don't know. Did you go to ionosphere as well? I was flying what was called the mud mover. I spent most of my time

58:42.458 --> 58:52.500
[SPEAKER_00]: hugging the ground. I think that's where the most excitement is, right? Low flying aircraft, so most of the flying was below 300 feet. Ah, damn,

58:52.580 --> 59:00.964
[SPEAKER_01]: that is what, 100 meters or what, 70 meters? How much is that in meters? I think nearly 80-85% of the flying was below 300 feet. Are you kidding me?

59:01.104 --> 59:09.809
[SPEAKER_01]: That is exciting. Did you ever break the sonic boom at that level? Yeah, but in the air you don't hear anything. Just a minor move of the aircraft,

59:09.849 --> 59:17.678
[SPEAKER_00]: that's it, nothing. Let's talk about the animals on the ground. They must be feeling the wrath of your afterbites. If you hear a sonic boom,

59:17.878 --> 59:28.007
[SPEAKER_00]: it's really loud. It can shake you up. It can shadow windows depending on which part of the sonic boom you're in. Are you allowed to break sonic

59:28.027 --> 59:39.576
[SPEAKER_01]: boom that low? There are certain profiles where you would do it. But for doing that, there are certain specified areas. So when you go sonic or

59:39.596 --> 59:49.060
[SPEAKER_00]: supersonic, The shockwave doesn't hit people's houses and creates a lot of racket. It can be disconcerting, you know. But Ashutosh, one

59:49.100 --> 59:59.087
[SPEAKER_01]: flight that has stuck with you until now and the reason behind it. I've had many interesting flights. Some had scary aspects and some were not.

59:59.207 --> 01:00:07.729
[SPEAKER_00]: But I think one flight from my Air Force days, which I'll never forget, was during the Gargoyle War. which took place in the mountains in

01:00:07.749 --> 01:00:16.811
[SPEAKER_00]: Kashmir. For our international audience, this is a small conflict that happened in a region. Small for the world, but fairly large for the ones

01:00:16.871 --> 01:00:26.814
[SPEAKER_01]: involved in it. But this was a skirmish, or you can say a war. I don't think skirmish is the right way to put it in respect to armies that fought from

01:00:26.894 --> 01:00:35.356
[SPEAKER_01]: both ends of the countries. But yeah, this is something that the subcontinent saw back in two decades, I think. But yeah, please

01:00:35.396 --> 01:00:43.493
[SPEAKER_01]: continue, Ashutosh. All of a sudden it became quite apparent that some areas we had not been paying much attention to, all the mountains up

01:00:43.573 --> 01:00:53.857
[SPEAKER_00]: north and the valleys there, we hadn't been flying inside them deep enough to handle targets which are very small, very obscure, not easy to

01:00:53.957 --> 01:01:01.516
[SPEAKER_00]: visually spot. Not only that by day, but by night. I remember doing this very interesting profile with my commanding officer. Two of us went and

01:01:01.536 --> 01:01:10.900
[SPEAKER_00]: did this at night. And we went through these valleys at low level. We didn't have any night vision goggles, no infrared pods,

01:01:11.080 --> 01:01:20.143
[SPEAKER_00]: forward-looking infrared pods and things like that to guide us. And this entire flying was done visual. And the intention was to see at what

01:01:20.183 --> 01:01:27.702
[SPEAKER_00]: speeds, what heights are you able to visually acquire certain kinds of terrain and targets and things. And I remember doing this flight with

01:01:27.742 --> 01:01:37.046
[SPEAKER_00]: him lasting nearly about one hour in which we were flying at about 500 feet agile in the night only some moonlight visible with mountains on

01:01:37.126 --> 01:01:46.790
[SPEAKER_00]: both sides of us rising up nearly 15 to 18 000 feet above us and we were following the contour of the valley the two of us i could only see his

01:01:47.550 --> 01:01:54.289
[SPEAKER_00]: auntie called once in a while in front of me A very harrowing flight, visually avoiding terrain, flying nearly a thousand kilometers per

01:01:54.309 --> 01:02:01.371
[SPEAKER_00]: hour, close to the ground at night, you know, constantly whipping the aircraft left, whipping the aircraft right, no infrared, no nothing.

01:02:02.191 --> 01:02:10.273
[SPEAKER_00]: That was quite an interesting fight for me as far as fighter flying is concerned. And I think as far as paragliding is concerned, an

01:02:10.293 --> 01:02:18.442
[SPEAKER_00]: interesting fight would be the fact that in the last four flights that me and my wife flew together, I flew in formation with her. We would catch up

01:02:18.562 --> 01:02:24.745
[SPEAKER_00]: over the landing ground. I would fly with her in what would be called an echelon position. We were slightly offset to one side and slightly

01:02:24.785 --> 01:02:32.928
[SPEAKER_00]: behind at an angle of about 30 degrees. And all these four flights, I flew with her for about 10 to 15 minutes, holding that position while she was

01:02:32.948 --> 01:02:40.891
[SPEAKER_00]: just flying around and I would just hold position and fly around. And we did that formation flying for her last four flights. So that's the

01:02:40.911 --> 01:02:43.452
[SPEAKER_00]: interesting thing I remember from flying with her.

01:02:46.730 --> 01:02:57.512
[SPEAKER_01]: I think proximity flying and wingmanning in general, it's mesmerizing. Sharing the joy with another human that understands it

01:02:58.092 --> 01:03:07.874
[SPEAKER_01]: the same way is quite exceptional. And you seem to have lived quite a fulfilling life in aviation, Ashutosh. Yeah, man, I'm just short for

01:03:07.914 --> 01:03:16.277
[SPEAKER_01]: words. I don't know. It's just nice talking to you about these things. And this is something that I wanted. to do for so long. For our audiences

01:03:16.317 --> 01:03:26.687
[SPEAKER_01]: who don't know, Ashutosh also happens to be a mentor for me here at Paragliding Atlas and is a big force behind what is coming to you in the

01:03:26.707 --> 01:03:38.718
[SPEAKER_01]: show. And so needless to say, I think there's endless ways we can keep this episode going, but I would love to bring out more awesomeness with

01:03:38.738 --> 01:03:48.967
[SPEAKER_01]: you in tandem and in formation flying, in wingmanning, and in podcasting, in whichever way possible. But for now, I think we have hit

01:03:49.007 --> 01:03:58.475
[SPEAKER_01]: the nail on the head for what we planned for. Thank you so much, Ashutosh. Thank you so much for having me on your show and I wish my very artists all

